<p>A Victorian polymath who turned his father's utilitarianism into something more livable</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/articles-79891_thumbnail.thumb_ifacebook.jpg" alt="Photograph of John Stuart Mill, c. 1870." width="860" height="320" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Mill's life was a long argument with the system he was raised in.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    The most over-educated child in England
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>John Stuart Mill (1806–1873) was raised by his father, James Mill, as an experiment in the philosophical education of a single child. The result was the most precocious intellectual upbringing in nineteenth-century Britain, and possibly anywhere. By age three Mill was reading Greek. By eight he had read most of Plato's dialogues in the original. By twelve he had been put through formal logic and political economy. By his early teens he was reading manuscript drafts of his father's books and helping with the editing.</p>
<p>This was not random precocity. James Mill and his close friend Jeremy Bentham wanted to demonstrate that the right kind of education could produce a fully rational adult, ready to carry forward the reformist project they called Philosophic Radicalism. The younger Mill was their test case. He passed by every external measure: a stellar career at the East India Company, a long list of books and essays, and, eventually, a seat in Parliament.</p>
<p>The internal cost was severe. At twenty, Mill suffered what he later called a "mental crisis": a deep depression in which the goals he had been raised to pursue no longer felt worth pursuing. He came out of it gradually, helped by the poetry of Wordsworth, conversation with people his father would have considered intellectually frivolous, and his own slow recognition that he had been taught to reason but not to feel. The experience permanently altered his sense of what a good human life requires. It also altered his utilitarianism. Bentham had thought all pleasures were of the same kind, differing only in quantity. Mill, after the breakdown, became convinced that some kinds of pleasure are qualitatively higher than others, and that a utilitarianism worth defending would have to recognize this.</p>
<p>In 1830 he met Harriet Taylor, an intellectually formidable woman who was unhappily married. For two decades they conducted a deep but officially platonic relationship that scandalized Mill's social circle. After her husband's death in 1849 they finally married. Harriet Taylor Mill died in 1858, just three years later. Mill spent the rest of his life publishing the works they had planned together, including <em>On Liberty</em>, <em>Utilitarianism</em>, and <em>The Subjection of Women</em>. He always credited her as a co-author, more emphatically than his contemporaries believed her contributions warranted; some recent scholarship has come around to thinking he was probably right.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    The world Mill was writing for
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Mill is the first thinker in this course who lived after the Industrial Revolution, and you can feel it in his work. Aristotle wrote for citizens of a small Greek city. Aquinas wrote for an educated clergy in medieval Christendom. Kant wrote in a Prussian university town that, by his own admission, had not changed much in centuries. Mill writes from the center of the world's first industrial society, in the middle of arguments about poverty, factory conditions, women's rights, colonial administration, and electoral reform. Ethics, for him, is not an academic exercise. It is the framework you need to make political decisions in a country that is changing faster than its inherited moral vocabulary can describe.</p>
<p>This is one of the reasons Mill is so concerned with applicability. He wants a moral theory that you can actually use to decide between policies, to evaluate institutions, to weigh competing demands. Kant's categorical imperative, on his view, is too thin to do this work; it gives you a check on certain kinds of action (no lying, no false promising) but no way to compare two policies neither of which involves any lying at all. Utilitarianism, Mill thinks, provides what you need: a common measure (happiness) by which any policy can be assessed, and a clear test (does it produce more happiness than the alternatives?) that anyone can apply.</p>
<p>The intellectual setting also matters. Mill is writing in the wake of Bentham, in conversation with Coleridge and the German romantics, and against the backdrop of a rapidly developing British empiricist tradition. His own great work in logic and epistemology, the <em>System of Logic</em> (1843), is an attempt to ground all human knowledge in inductive methods drawn from the natural sciences. <em>Utilitarianism</em> is the moral application of that broader empiricist program. If knowledge of the world comes from observation and inference, then moral knowledge must come from observation of what makes lives go well or badly, and inference from there to general principles. There is no separate moral faculty, no innate principles given in advance. Whatever morality is, it has to be something we discover by attending to creatures like us in the world we actually inhabit.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    What Utilitarianism is trying to do
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>The book has a deliberately defensive shape. Mill wrote it in the early 1860s, by which point utilitarianism had been around for two generations and had attracted a long list of criticisms. Some of the objections were philosophical (utilitarianism cannot account for justice; the principle of utility cannot be proved; happiness is not the kind of thing moral theory should track). Some were polemical (utilitarianism is a doctrine fit only for swine; it makes you cold and calculating; it reduces moral seriousness to bookkeeping). Mill wants to answer all of them in a short book aimed at general readers.</p>
<p>The result is a book that does several things at once. It states the Greatest Happiness Principle and tries to make it precise. It responds to a series of standard objections and tries to defuse them. It offers a famous "proof" of the principle of utility. And it ends with an extended treatment of justice, partly because justice is where consequentialist theories are most often thought to fail. Each chapter has its own job, and the chapters are not quite the same kind of argument.</p>
<p>Reading the book well means tracking what Mill is doing in each chapter rather than expecting a single linear argument. Chapter 1 sets up the project. Chapter 2 is the substantive core: this is where utilitarianism gets defined and most of the work gets done. Chapters 3 and 4 are detours into supporting questions (motivation, then proof). Chapter 5 is the longest chapter and works through the most resistant case, justice.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
