<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">5</span>
    A roadmap to the reading
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Here is what to expect in each chapter.</p>
<p><strong>Chapter 1: General Remarks.</strong> A short preface. Mill explains why moral philosophy has made so little progress despite millennia of effort, sets up the question he will try to answer, and acknowledges that he cannot offer a strict logical proof of any moral principle. Skim this chapter on a first pass; the substantive work begins in Chapter 2.</p>
<p><strong>Chapter 2: What Utilitarianism Is.</strong> The heart of the book. Mill states the Greatest Happiness Principle, distinguishes higher and lower pleasures, and works through the main objections to utilitarianism: that it is a doctrine fit for swine, that happiness is not the right standard, that we cannot calculate consequences, that it makes morality too demanding, and so on. If you read carefully nowhere else in the book, read carefully here.</p>
<p><strong>Chapter 3: Of the Ultimate Sanction of the Principle of Utility.</strong> Mill turns from <em>what is right</em> to <em>what motivates people to do right</em>. His answer distinguishes external sanctions (legal penalties, social pressure, divine sanction) from internal sanctions (conscience, sympathy). He argues that the internal sanctions are what really do the moral work, and that they are rooted in human sociability rather than in any rational insight.</p>
<p><strong>Chapter 4: Of What Sort of Proof the Principle of Utility Is Susceptible.</strong> Mill's notorious "proof." He concedes that the principle of utility cannot be proven in the strict logical sense but claims he can offer something close to a proof. The argument he gives has been controversial since the day the book was published; one specific move in it is almost universally regarded as a serious error, and we will spend some time on that move in the lecture page.</p>
<p><strong>Chapter 5: Of the Connexion Between Justice and Utility.</strong> The longest and most substantial chapter. Mill takes up the hardest case for any consequentialist theory: justice. Why does it feel wrong to frame an innocent person even when doing so would maximize happiness? Why do rights feel like absolute claims rather than rules of thumb? Mill's answer is that justice is a special class of utility, marked off by particularly strong feelings of resentment when it is violated. Whether his answer succeeds is one of the great debates in modern ethics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
